{
  "title": "Australian housing valuation",
  "asOf": {
    "priceToIncome": "2026-Q1",
    "priceToRent": "2025-Q3",
    "q": "2026-Q2"
  },
  "generated": "2026-11-12",
  "definitions": {
    "priceToIncome": "OECD nominal house price index for Australia divided by ABS average weekly earnings, all employees, persons. The numerator is an index, so the level is index points per dollar of weekly earnings.",
    "priceToRent": "OECD nominal house price index divided by the OECD rent price index. The ratio is the inverse of the gross rental yield and the housing equivalent of a price-to-earnings multiple. A net cap rate after vacancy, operating costs and depreciation would be lower, but the expense ratio is stable over long periods, so the ranking is similar.",
    "qRatio": "Owner equity in the dwelling stock divided by the replacement cost of the dwelling structure. Equity is the market value of residential land and dwellings less housing credit. Land is in the numerator. A Q of 1 is the mark where equity equals structure replacement cost.",
    "qDetrended": "The raw Q divided by its running geometric mean, using every observation to that date.",
    "ladder": "Each row holds earnings, rents, housing credit and replacement costs at their current levels and moves only the price of the dwelling stock. The Q numerator moves with the price; the replacement cost is fixed. The 0% row uses 2026-Q1 for price-to-income, 2025-Q3 for price-to-rent and 2026-Q2 for the Q."
  },
